# Personal Claude Code Instructions

These rules apply to ALL sessions across ALL projects and languages.
They override default behavior unless a project-specific CLAUDE.md
narrows them.

## Verification Before Claiming

- Never invent numbers, file counts, char limits, plugin existence,
  version strings, or config keys.
- If unsure whether a plugin/file/config exists, check first with
  `ls`, `cat`, `gh api`, or the official docs.
- When citing Claude Code behavior (settings keys, slash commands,
  hook events, skill mechanics), link to or quote the official docs.
- Distinguish "the docs say X" from "I think X" explicitly. State
  your confidence.

## Avoid Over-Engineering

- Prefer the simplest solution that works. Three similar lines beats
  a premature abstraction.
- Do not introduce new components, abstractions, helpers, CSS classes,
  version bumps, or backward-compat shims unless explicitly requested.
- When given multiple viable options, recommend ONE with a one-line
  trade-off. No multi-option matrices unless asked.
- Do not modify version strings, lock files, or unrelated configs
  beyond what the task requires.
- A bug fix doesn't need surrounding cleanup. A one-shot doesn't need
  a helper.

## Git & Worktree Discipline

- Never auto-create git worktrees unless explicitly requested.
- Always verify the current branch with `git branch --show-current`
  before amending or committing.
- Verify branch names from actual git state (`git branch -a`), not
  from README references or memory.
- Prefer new commits over `--amend` unless explicitly asked.
- Standard `git push` (without `--force` / `--force-with-lease`) is
  the **normal workflow** — including direct pushes to `main` on
  personal/solo repos. No special approval is required beyond the
  user's intent to commit and push.

### Force-push policy (only `--force` / `--force-with-lease` flags)

This section concerns **only force-push variants**, NOT standard
`git push`.

- **`git push --force` is strictly forbidden.** Never use it. Always
  prefer `--force-with-lease` — it refuses to push if the remote
  has changed since your last fetch, protecting against data loss.

- **`git push --force-with-lease` is allowed ONLY** in two scenarios,
  and only when the user explicitly requests the action:
  1. Reverting / dropping a local commit the user asked to undo.
  2. Rebasing local history (interactive rebase, squash, reword)
     that the user explicitly requested.

- **Default rule** : new need → new commit. Never rewrite existing
  history just because something needs to change. History is
  append-only by default.

## Platform

This machine is macOS. For shell/script work specifics
(`/bin/bash` 3.2 vs Homebrew bash 5+, BSD vs GNU commands), the
`mac-platform` skill loads on demand for shell/setup tasks.

## When in doubt

Ask ONE focused question rather than guess. Cite a file path or grep
output to ground the question.
